 8-13 despite a D-favorable midterm environment"], "rationale": "Alabama's deep Republican lean, Tuberville's dominant fundraising, unanimous Solid/Safe R ratings from all seven raters, and market prices under 10% for Jones all point the same direction. Even granting Jones a favorable midterm environment and his crossover-voter pitch, the limited polling shows a high-single-digit to low-double-digit Republican lead.", "watch": "A stronger-than-expected midterm wave, a Tuberville misstep, or hard evidence of sustained Republican crossover to Jones could narrow the margin, though Jones would need far more movement than current polls show.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Kay Ivey (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Alabama's open governor's race pits Democrat Doug Jones, a former Senator, against Republican Senator Tommy Tuberville to succeed term-limited Kay Ivey. The state's R+15 partisan lean frames the contest, and every major race rater — Cook Political Report, Inside Elections, Sabato's Crystal Ball, Silver Bulletin, and others — calls it Solid or Safe Republican. Betting markets give Jones roughly a one-in-twelve chance, pricing the race well short of competitive.\n\nMoney and polling point the same direction. Tuberville has raised about $14.5 million to Jones's $4.8 million and enters the fall with a two-to-one cash advantage. Public polls are sparse but consistently show Tuberville ahead, by eight points in July and thirteen in late September. Jones has campaigned on rising costs and claims crossover Republican support, and a favorable midterm environment offers some hope, but the weight of the evidence points to a comfortable Republican hold in November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0254, "margin": -12.37}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0512, "margin": -12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0161, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0141, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0131, "m